---
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import SocialLinks from '../components/SocialLinks.astro';
import ProjectCard from '../components/ProjectCard.astro';

const featured = (await getCollection('projects'))
  .filter((p) => p.data.featured)
  .sort((a, b) => a.data.order - b.data.order);
---

<Base
  title="Robbe De Win"
  description="Bioinformatician in Leuven, Belgium. Genome-scale metabolic modelling, sequencing pipelines, and the tooling around them."
>
  <h1>Robbe De Win</h1>

  <p class="lede">
    I build computational pipelines and the tooling that keeps them honest. I
    finished a Master of Bioinformatics at KU Leuven in 2026, after a Master of
    Biomedical Sciences — two theses, one on genome-scale metabolic modelling
    across roughly 1,500 bacterial genomes, one on allele-specific expression in
    treatment-resistant leukemia.
  </p>

  <p>
    What draws me is the point where biology stops being a wet-lab question and
    becomes an engineering one: a thousand genomes is not a bigger version of one
    genome, it is a different problem. Most of what I have learned came from
    getting a pipeline of that size to run reliably, validating its output against
    experimental data, and being able to say afterwards exactly how a number was
    produced.
  </p>

  <p>
    The same habit shows up outside biology. I built a financial planning engine
    for Belgian households because the generic advice stops working exactly where
    the local tax rules start, and a bike garage that computes its own component
    wear from ride data because chains fail on mileage and I kept guessing. Both
    taught me more about data modelling than any course did.
  </p>

  <p>
    I am currently looking for a first role in bioinformatics or computational
    biology — Flanders, Brussels, or remote from Leuven. Dutch and English.
    The quickest way to reach me is email.
  </p>

  <SocialLinks />

  <h2 class="section-label">Selected work</h2>
  <ul class="card-list">
    {featured.map((p) => (
      <ProjectCard
        href={`/projects/${p.id}`}
        title={p.data.title}
        summary={p.data.summary}
        tags={p.data.tags}
      />
    ))}
  </ul>
  <p style="margin-top:1.2rem"><a href="/projects">All projects →</a></p>
</Base>
